with just days to go until the german  election on sunday outgoing chancellor  angela merkel has hit the campaign trail  with her would-be successor armin lashit  it is the last minute bid to boost his  popularity with voters  merkel and lashit visited the  northeastern city of strausend where  merkel first won her bundestag seat back  in 1990.  now after 16 years as chancellor  merkel remains very popular in germany  in contrast lashit who wants to take  over the top job  has struggled to connect with voters  he's badly in need of a boost  and let's get more now we are joined by  political correspondent anja koch anya  how much of a boost could markle give  lashed especially at this late stage in  the campaign  well the cdu is certainly hoping that it  will be a boost  for amin lashed what's remarkable is  that this has been one of the very rare  appearances of angela merkel and army  lashed together  during this election campaign it hasn't  happened very often that we see that we  have seen the two of them together on  one stage angela merkel for most of the  election campaign  had stayed out of the campaign that was  met with criticism within her own party  within the cdu but also within uh from  within her sister party the csu so  angela megan now stepping up and  campaigning together for uh together  with amin lashed and for amin  certainly meant um certainly meant to  give him a boost  what is the mood anya because i mean  miracles conservatives they have edged  up a percentage point in the polls but  they're still lagging behind their main  rivals the social democrats the election  now just in four days time  are they worried  well in his public statements and  interviews mean luscious stresses that  he is still confident and that he is  optimistic that the cbu will win this  election and that he is going to be  germany's next chancellor but it is  however obvious that the party is  worried and they have all reason to be  to be honest i mean has had a very hard  time connecting with voters during the  selection campaign and when you look at  the opinion polls depending on which  poll you look at the cdu at the moment  is around 22  while the social democrats who no one  really saw as a real danger to the cdu  just months ago is polling at around 25  now that is a very small margin but it  shows you that the cdu can't be sure to  win this election and it's a really big  question in fact as to whether how much  we should read into those numbers anya  because polls show as much as 40 percent  of the electorate are still undecided a  really big number there first of all why  is that and do we have any indication in  terms of where those voters might swing  well that certainly has to do with the  rather unique situation that germany is  facing at the moment for years and  decades german voters  were used to having a chancellor who is  re-running for office  often with a so-called chancellor bonus  because some voters tend to vote  for people who they think they know well  this time it's different we are having  three candidates that have never been  chancellor before so it's kind of more  unpredictable of what to expect this is  not something that german voters  particularly like they like things more  predictable well this time they are less  predictable so it might just take voters  more time to decide political  correspondent anyakoch thank you  local candidates are also campaigning  hard to win voters especially those who  feel that they are not often listened to  dw reporter carolina machaus traveled to  offenbach in western germany where one  young social democrat is determined to  give minorities a voice in politics  so the plan is we'll take our good vibes  with us and go down to the river and try  to strike up a conversation with people  noor khauser is 21 years old  she's a member of the social democrats  and has a seat in the local council  she wants german politics to become more  diverse  hiba was born in a refugee shelter in  germany after her parents fled religious  persecution in pakistan  she lives in offenbach in western  germany  over 60 percent of the residents here  have a mixed background but only about  one in four of them has german  citizenship and the right to vote  on average they earn less than  non-immigrants  the education system is a challenge as  is the labor market  [Music]  there's a general election on september  26th are you going to vote  can i ask you what the most pressing  issues are for you in germany  the man answers in turkish one of  hibba's colleagues translates  we are blue collar workers we want our  salaries to reflect price increases  groceries have become more expensive so  our salary should rise too  are you voting for spd  the conversations on the street continue  i grew up here i'm from offenbach but i  can't vote because i only have a turkish  passport yeah it means decisions are  made and you can't really have a say  i think it sucks to be honest  what's your name  hippa do you say it like that  with b  in germany over 10 million residents  don't have german citizenship and so  don't have the right to vote in the  federal election although they live work  and pay taxes here and those who have  the right to vote often don't one reason  for that is a very one-sided election  campaign  the problem is always that people aren't  reached out to that's not often done  many speak to a very specific group who  they think are the ones who definitely  have the right to vote it's mainly white  people who they approach but for example  no attention is paid to people who look  like me but you can see that the  conversation works when you talk to  people they're open to it and share  their problems  it's raining outside so the group takes  a break  many here believe that racism can only  be countered if politics is more open to  immigrants  you do something for society you really  involve yourself you're politically  engaged still you always hear